---
/**
 * Original BotPlanet explainer — what a mower's slope rating actually means.
 *
 * THE MISTAKE THIS EXISTS TO STOP. Every mower in the category is rated in
 * PERCENT and almost every buyer reads it as DEGREES. "45% slope" sounds like
 * something close to a cliff and is 24 degrees; a 100% slope is 45 degrees,
 * not vertical. Somebody who reads 45% as 45° concludes their garden is far
 * too steep and buys nothing, or assumes a 30% machine will cope with a bank
 * that is actually 30° — which is 58% and beyond every mower we hold.
 *
 * Three paragraphs of trigonometry do not fix that. One right-angled triangle
 * with both numbers on it does, which is the entire argument for drawing this
 * rather than writing it.
 *
 * The figures are arithmetic, not claims: percent = rise ÷ run × 100, degrees
 * = arctan(rise ÷ run). Nothing here is attributed to a manufacturer and no
 * machine is named.
 */
---
<figure class="dgm">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 640 300" width="100%" role="img"
    aria-label="A right-angled triangle showing that a slope of 45 percent is about 24 degrees, that 58 percent is 30 degrees, and that 100 percent is 45 degrees rather than vertical.">
    <rect x="0" y="0" width="640" height="300" rx="14" fill="var(--panel)" stroke="var(--border)"/>

    <text x="28" y="38" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#f5f5f7">Percent is not degrees</text>
    <text x="28" y="58" font-size="11.5" fill="#c9c9d1">Mowers are rated in percent. A 45% slope is about 24°, not 45°.</text>

    <!-- ground line -->
    <line x1="60" y1="240" x2="440" y2="240" stroke="var(--border-2)" stroke-width="2"/>
    <text x="230" y="262" font-size="11" fill="#8b8b95">run — 100 across</text>

    <!-- the 45% slope: rise 45 over run 100 -->
    <path d="M60 240 L440 240 L440 69 Z" fill="rgba(124,147,255,0.10)" stroke="none"/>
    <line x1="60" y1="240" x2="440" y2="69" stroke="#7c93ff" stroke-width="3"/>
    <line x1="440" y1="240" x2="440" y2="69" stroke="var(--border-2)" stroke-width="2"/>
    {/* Inside the triangle, not outside it. Set to the right of the vertical
        these two lines ran straight into the ladder of equivalents. */}
    <text x="430" y="150" font-size="11" fill="#8b8b95" text-anchor="end">rise</text>
    <text x="430" y="166" font-size="11" fill="#8b8b95" text-anchor="end">45 up</text>

    {/* Angle marker: a real arc swept from the ground line to the hypotenuse at
        r=70 about the origin, rather than the stub that was here. The endpoint
        is 70 units along the unit vector of (380, −171). */}
    <path d="M130 240 A 70 70 0 0 0 123.8 211.3" fill="none" stroke="#7c93ff" stroke-width="2"/>
    {/* BELOW the hypotenuse. Set above it, the slope line ran through the
        middle of the words and struck out the one number the drawing is for. */}
    <text x="146" y="226" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" fill="#7c93ff">24° = 45%</text>

    <!-- the ladder of equivalents -->
    <g>
      <text x="480" y="96" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#f5f5f7">What the ratings mean</text>

      <rect x="480" y="110" width="132" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--border-2)"/>
      <text x="490" y="127" font-size="11.5" fill="#c9c9d1">30% &#8594; 17°</text>

      <rect x="480" y="142" width="132" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--border-2)"/>
      <text x="490" y="159" font-size="11.5" fill="#c9c9d1">45% &#8594; 24°</text>

      <rect x="480" y="174" width="132" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--border-2)"/>
      <text x="490" y="191" font-size="11.5" fill="#c9c9d1">58% &#8594; 30°</text>

      <rect x="480" y="206" width="132" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--border-2)"/>
      <text x="490" y="223" font-size="11.5" fill="#c9c9d1">80% &#8594; 39°</text>

      <rect x="480" y="238" width="132" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--panel-2)" stroke="var(--border-2)"/>
      <text x="490" y="255" font-size="11.5" fill="#c9c9d1">100% &#8594; 45°</text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <figcaption class="note">
    Slope in percent is rise divided by run. A 100% slope rises one metre for every metre across,
    which is 45° — not vertical. Measure your bank in percent before comparing it with a rating.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<style>
  .dgm { margin: 1.75rem 0; }
  .dgm svg { max-width: 640px; display: block; }
  .dgm .note { margin-top: 8px; }
</style>
